WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.895 --> 00:00:01.495
I'm Dr.

2
00:00:00.448 --> 00:00:06.208
Richard Halvorsen, a general surgeon at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:06.208 --> 00:00:10.530
Laura Hemmingsen asked me to talk about
replacing our EHR before anybody plans

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.835
anything, so here it is.

5
00:00:11.835 --> 00:00:14.666
I'll be blunt, because somebody should be.

6
00:00:14.666 --> 00:00:21.170
I have been here twenty-two years and I have
lived through two of these "upgrades."

7
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:26.084
Every time, the vendor shows us a demo where
everything takes three clicks,

8
00:00:26.084 --> 00:00:28.813
and then we go live and it takes thirty.

9
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:32.458
When Plexmoor came in, nobody asked a single
surgeon how we actually work.

10
00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:33.720
They asked IT.

11
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.225
So my operative notes got longer and my
time with patients got shorter.

12
00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:39.459
Here's a typical day.

13
00:00:39.459 --> 00:00:40.888
I'm in the building by six.

14
00:00:40.888 --> 00:00:44.937
I round on my post-op patients, then I'm
in the OR most of the day.

15
00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:49.747
Between cases I'm signing orders,
answering messages from the floor,

16
00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:51.271
and reviewing labs.

17
00:00:51.271 --> 00:00:53.278
After my last case I see
patients in clinic.

18
00:00:53.278 --> 00:00:56.476
The documentation doesn't stop
when the surgery stops.

19
00:00:56.476 --> 00:01:02.307
An operative note in Plexmoor has fields I
have to click through even when they

20
00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:07.004
don't apply, and the order sets were built years
ago for somebody else's workflow.

21
00:01:07.004 --> 00:01:09.073
So I work around them.

22
00:01:09.073 --> 00:01:12.401
I keep my own templates in a text
file and paste them in.

23
00:01:12.401 --> 00:01:14.669
I'm not proud of that, but it's faster.

24
00:01:14.669 --> 00:01:17.775
What worries me most about the change?

25
00:01:17.775 --> 00:01:20.584
More clicks.

26
00:01:20.584 --> 00:01:21.845
Less time with patients.

27
00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:25.727
Most nights I'm still finishing charts
after my kids are in bed,

28
00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:27.189
and I'm not the only one.

29
00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:32.459
Two of my partners have cut back their hours,
and the charting is part of why.

30
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:37.394
If the new system adds ten minutes a day
per surgeon, that's real.

31
00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:41.462
That's a patient I don't talk
to long enough,

32
00:01:41.462 --> 00:01:43.115
or a note I sign without
reading it carefully.

33
00:01:43.115 --> 00:01:46.761
And during go-live, it will be worse
before it gets better.

34
00:01:46.761 --> 00:01:48.110
I've seen it.

35
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.123
Nobody asked the surgeons last time.

36
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:54.541
This time, give surgeons a real say in
how the screens are built.

37
00:01:54.541 --> 00:01:56.282
Not a survey.

38
00:01:56.282 --> 00:02:00.903
A seat at the table where the order sets and
the note templates get designed.

39
00:02:00.903 --> 00:02:05.215
I can give an hour a week, but it has
to be early, before cases,

40
00:02:05.215 --> 00:02:08.232
around six-thirty in the morning.

41
00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:10.236
If the meetings are at two in
the afternoon,

42
00:02:10.236 --> 00:02:16.591
you've already decided surgeons won't come,
and then you'll say we didn't care.

43
00:02:16.591 --> 00:02:20.714
And don't send me a glossy slide deck
from Orivane or anybody else.

44
00:02:20.714 --> 00:02:24.914
Show me a surgeon at a hospital like
ours who uses it every day,

45
00:02:24.914 --> 00:02:26.839
and let me ask him the hard questions.

46
00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:29.387
How long does an op note take?

47
00:02:29.387 --> 00:02:33.903
What happens when the system is slow
at seven in the morning?

48
00:02:33.903 --> 00:02:36.873
Did they get their evenings back, or not?

49
00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:38.185
I'll believe a colleague.

50
00:02:38.185 --> 00:02:41.685
I won't believe a salesperson.

51
00:02:41.685 --> 00:02:46.430
Some hospitals are using AI tools that listen
to a visit and draft the note.

52
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.030
Maybe.

53
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.039
I've read about them.

54
00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:52.473
If it saves me time and it's
accurate, I'm interested.

55
00:02:52.473 --> 00:02:54.032
But I sign that note.

56
00:02:54.032 --> 00:02:58.825
If it gets something wrong, that's my
name on it, not the software's.

57
00:02:58.825 --> 00:03:03.784
So I'd want to try it on real cases, with
a way to check what it heard,

58
00:03:03.784 --> 00:03:06.442
before anybody tells me it's ready.

59
00:03:06.442 --> 00:03:11.503
What would make me an advocate,
not just a participant?

60
00:03:11.503 --> 00:03:12.103
Results.

61
00:03:11.503 --> 00:03:16.231
If the people who build this listen, and my
note time actually goes down,

62
00:03:16.231 --> 00:03:19.981
I'll say so in the medical staff meeting.

63
00:03:19.981 --> 00:03:21.576
Surgeons listen to surgeons.

64
00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:24.435
But I won't sell something I
haven't seen work.

65
00:03:24.435 --> 00:03:25.727
You'd have to earn that.

66
00:03:25.727 --> 00:03:30.206
And here's how to know whether it's
working for surgeons.

67
00:03:30.206 --> 00:03:32.021
Measure time.

68
00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:36.931
How long the op note takes, how many minutes
we spend in the chart after hours,

69
00:03:36.931 --> 00:03:38.697
before and after go-live.

70
00:03:38.697 --> 00:03:39.297
The system can track that.

71
00:03:38.697 --> 00:03:42.040
Then show us the numbers, good or bad.

